WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.076 --> 00:00:12.765
Good morning.

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:21.040
For two or three of you out
there who don't know me,

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:23.830
I'm Bill Hecht class of '61.

00:00:23.830 --> 00:00:29.320
And I'm your senior employee as
head of the Alumni Association.

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:32.750
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:38.600
You might wonder how a
guy from the class of '61

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:40.770
got a red coat.

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:45.330
A number of years
ago the class of '24,

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:48.710
which is celebrating
its 70th this year,

00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:52.160
had some issues that
needed settling.

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:55.970
And somehow or other I was able
to settle them for that class.

00:00:55.970 --> 00:00:58.430
And as a great
privilege and tribute

00:00:58.430 --> 00:01:01.130
they made me an honorary
member of that class,

00:01:01.130 --> 00:01:03.530
insisting that I always
wear a freshman tie.

00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:10.280
So I now have my own two
connections, a six and a one.

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:14.420
My graduate degree was in '76
and my undergraduate in '61.

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:17.390
And '24 which I'm
just as proud of.

00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:19.460
And I'm busting
my buttons to say

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:22.840
that in '95 my daughter will
get a master's from this place,

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:26.980
so I'll have another
MIT connection.

00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:29.990
I wonder if you all know that
last night's pops was not only

00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:32.360
a very special night for
us, but it was actually

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:37.349
a test for the potential
new conductor of the pops.

00:01:37.349 --> 00:01:39.305
[LAUGHTER]

00:01:40.283 --> 00:01:43.706
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:50.880
It is said that he practiced
Arthur Fiedler's hand motions

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:53.160
studiously before the mirror.

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:54.630
And I think that
I'm going to take

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:57.000
on the job of being his
agent and represent him,

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:59.208
because I think we can get
a hell of a good contract.

00:02:01.179 --> 00:02:02.970
Finally, it's my great
pleasure in addition

00:02:02.970 --> 00:02:07.560
to welcoming you all here
today to introduce Chuck Vest.

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:10.470
Chuck is president
of MIT as you know.

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:13.590
He came to us about
four years ago.

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:16.860
And he may be a
little wistful today.

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:18.840
As you know, many
people who come

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:23.370
in the fall of four years ago
graduated about a week ago.

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:26.580
His class, the class
of '94 did leave.

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:30.300
Chuck, I guess fortunately
for us, but perhaps not so

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:32.850
fortunately for him,
still remains with us.

00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:36.436
So let me introduce Chuck
Vest, president of MIT.

00:02:36.436 --> 00:02:39.908
[APPLAUSE]

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:46.130
Thank you very much, Bill.

00:02:46.130 --> 00:02:49.550
I'm just signed up for
the new five-year master

00:02:49.550 --> 00:02:50.750
of engineering program.

00:02:50.750 --> 00:02:52.320
That's all.

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:55.670
I've been for years
wanting to get up the nerve

00:02:55.670 --> 00:02:58.220
to ask Bill exactly
what this great favor he

00:02:58.220 --> 00:02:59.600
did for that class is.

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:01.970
But because I'm not
sure of its legality

00:03:01.970 --> 00:03:03.065
I've been afraid to ask.

00:03:03.065 --> 00:03:05.160
[LAUGHTER]

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:07.580
On a more serious note,
and a very happy one,

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:10.610
I understand that we
have a record number

00:03:10.610 --> 00:03:14.780
of alums and families here for
reunions on Technology Day.

00:03:14.780 --> 00:03:17.210
You are over 3,000 strong.

00:03:17.210 --> 00:03:19.220
My congratulations.

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:21.500
And we all have an
exciting day ahead of us.

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:24.332
[APPLAUSE]

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:29.340
The speakers for today's
program are truly

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:31.500
a stellar group
of men and women,

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:35.010
who will talk about art
and culture in the Academy,

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:37.860
and specifically at MIT.

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:41.880
Here at MIT, where the arts have
evolved over a hundred years

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:45.900
from a single extracurricular
activity in 1884,

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:51.150
the MIT Banjo Club, to a
panoply of artistic and cultural

00:03:51.150 --> 00:03:54.510
endeavors that you're going to
have a chance to sample here

00:03:54.510 --> 00:03:57.330
today in 1994.

00:03:57.330 --> 00:03:59.580
Special thanks go
to Jorge Rodriguez,

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:02.280
chairman of the
Technology Day Committee.

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:05.310
And to all the members of
the Technology Day Committee,

00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:07.620
the Council for the
Arts, for putting

00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:12.090
together such an astounding
and exciting program.

00:04:12.090 --> 00:04:14.290
I might add that the
watercolors reproduced

00:04:14.290 --> 00:04:17.010
in the Technology Day
poster was painted

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:19.920
by one of our graduate students
in the School of Architecture

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:23.310
and Planning, Lian Quan
Zhen, and that there

00:04:23.310 --> 00:04:28.940
are other examples of his work
on display today in the lobby.

00:04:28.940 --> 00:04:33.590
Now to some it might seem odd
that in this bastion of science

00:04:33.590 --> 00:04:37.610
and engineering we bring
together our alumni and alumni

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:40.250
to celebrate the arts.

00:04:40.250 --> 00:04:43.370
But most of you probably had
at least a brush with the arts

00:04:43.370 --> 00:04:48.140
at MIT, and many of you I'm
sure became more deeply involved

00:04:48.140 --> 00:04:52.040
in our music and
theater arts programs.

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:55.670
And now more than ever, our
students and our faculty

00:04:55.670 --> 00:05:00.060
are engaged in a wide range of
artistic and cultural pursuits.

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:04.640
In the visual arts, and
architecture, music, theater,

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:07.160
creative writing
and photography.

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:10.310
As well as in the emerging
media of interactive video,

00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:13.430
holography, and
hyperinstruments.

00:05:13.430 --> 00:05:17.040
Still, some of
you might ask why.

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:19.500
While it is true that
MIT is best known

00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:21.930
throughout the world for
the caliber of the engineers

00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:25.050
and scientists we send
out from this place,

00:05:25.050 --> 00:05:28.650
the Institute has in fact
produced a steady flow

00:05:28.650 --> 00:05:32.940
of artists, architects,
musicians, dancers, actors,

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:37.210
and others who are best known
for their artistic endeavors.

00:05:37.210 --> 00:05:40.350
We have string players and
wind players, composers

00:05:40.350 --> 00:05:43.710
and conductors in orchestras
around the country.

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:46.800
We have actors on
Broadway and in Hollywood.

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:50.310
And we have MIT graduates on
the faculties of music schools

00:05:50.310 --> 00:05:53.080
and many other universities.

00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:55.720
Of course, most MIT
students continue

00:05:55.720 --> 00:05:59.620
to pursue traditional careers
in engineering and science.

00:05:59.620 --> 00:06:03.250
But we are no less serious
about teaching music, theater,

00:06:03.250 --> 00:06:05.980
and the arts to these students.

00:06:05.980 --> 00:06:08.950
The arts, when taught
seriously and deeply,

00:06:08.950 --> 00:06:13.180
offer our students opportunities
to expand their imagination,

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:16.120
broaden their
perspective, and imagine

00:06:16.120 --> 00:06:19.080
ever-widening new possibilities.

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:21.540
This is true for all of us.

00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:24.660
When we develop our ability
to think creatively,

00:06:24.660 --> 00:06:26.520
we were able to
find new solutions

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:28.800
to the problems on
which we are working,

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:33.900
both in our laboratories and
in other aspects of our lives.

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:36.150
Take for example the
fields of engineering,

00:06:36.150 --> 00:06:39.070
and particularly
engineering education.

00:06:39.070 --> 00:06:42.340
We are moving very dramatically
toward a more integrative style

00:06:42.340 --> 00:06:45.940
of education, with
increased emphasis on design

00:06:45.940 --> 00:06:49.690
and synthesis, and a little
bit less emphasis on analysis

00:06:49.690 --> 00:06:52.140
and reduction in science.

00:06:52.140 --> 00:06:54.870
I think that art,
and an appreciation

00:06:54.870 --> 00:06:58.170
for the aesthetic components
of all that people do

00:06:58.170 --> 00:07:00.840
is very helpful in this regard.

00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:03.480
As we work to improve
the quality of designs

00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:06.210
and products, it's
going to be increasingly

00:07:06.210 --> 00:07:09.810
important for engineers
to co-operate with artists

00:07:09.810 --> 00:07:14.340
to tackle the visual, tactile,
and aesthetic qualities

00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:16.020
of their work.

00:07:16.020 --> 00:07:19.680
But quite aside from their role
in scientific and engineering

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:22.440
endeavors, the arts
should of course still

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:26.380
be an important part of our
lives and of our education.

00:07:26.380 --> 00:07:27.450
Why?

00:07:27.450 --> 00:07:30.000
Because they are
part of our humanity.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:32.850
They reflect our
human experience.

00:07:32.850 --> 00:07:34.470
The arts give us
a different kind

00:07:34.470 --> 00:07:36.930
of connection with
the world, and they

00:07:36.930 --> 00:07:39.960
can help us to understand the
many cultures that make up

00:07:39.960 --> 00:07:42.540
our community and our world.

00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:45.120
The arts allow people from
very different perspectives

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:48.330
and backgrounds to
communicate, and to interact

00:07:48.330 --> 00:07:52.180
with an almost build-in
degree of mutual respect.

00:07:52.180 --> 00:07:54.480
This is important
for our graduates

00:07:54.480 --> 00:07:58.380
as they take their skills into
an increasingly interdependent,

00:07:58.380 --> 00:08:01.560
global, and multicultural world.

00:08:01.560 --> 00:08:04.560
And this is very
important for MIT.

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:07.590
The more innovative we are in
celebrating and encouraging

00:08:07.590 --> 00:08:10.390
the richness and diversity
of our community,

00:08:10.390 --> 00:08:13.450
the better off
all of us will be.

00:08:13.450 --> 00:08:17.350
In some, the arts at MIT
are essential elements

00:08:17.350 --> 00:08:21.010
of our education and
of our community life.

00:08:21.010 --> 00:08:24.890
Not only are they healthy
here, they are thriving.

00:08:24.890 --> 00:08:28.300
This is great testimony to
the talent, the dedication,

00:08:28.300 --> 00:08:33.760
and the enthusiasm of a great
many faculty and students.

00:08:33.760 --> 00:08:37.150
And it is particular
testimony to one person

00:08:37.150 --> 00:08:40.120
who has done an extraordinary
job in stimulating

00:08:40.120 --> 00:08:42.760
and supporting the arts
since she arrived here

00:08:42.760 --> 00:08:46.300
at MIT in 1989.

00:08:46.300 --> 00:08:49.270
I refer, of course, to my
colleague Professor Ellen

00:08:49.270 --> 00:08:54.070
Harris, associate provost
for the arts at MIT.

00:08:54.070 --> 00:08:56.580
Ellen is a distinguished
scholar of music,

00:08:56.580 --> 00:09:00.460
who is also known widely
as a soprano soloist.

00:09:00.460 --> 00:09:03.370
She came to us from the
University of Chicago,

00:09:03.370 --> 00:09:05.320
where she was a professor
of music and head

00:09:05.320 --> 00:09:07.420
of the Department of Music.

00:09:07.420 --> 00:09:10.870
As a scholar, she has been
recognized for her work

00:09:10.870 --> 00:09:13.270
on the music of Handel.

00:09:13.270 --> 00:09:16.150
As a singer, she is best
known for her dramatic stage

00:09:16.150 --> 00:09:17.920
performances.

00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:20.950
Those of you who were here
for commencement last week

00:09:20.950 --> 00:09:22.870
and heard her sing
the national anthem

00:09:22.870 --> 00:09:25.690
know precisely what I mean.

00:09:25.690 --> 00:09:28.720
Ellen's arrival on campus
was the final fulfillment

00:09:28.720 --> 00:09:33.850
of a set of recommendations
made in 1987 by an arts review

00:09:33.850 --> 00:09:38.020
committee, which recommended the
appointment of a senior officer

00:09:38.020 --> 00:09:40.120
who would also be
a faculty member

00:09:40.120 --> 00:09:42.910
in either the performing
or visual arts.

00:09:42.910 --> 00:09:45.670
Someone to take
broad responsibility

00:09:45.670 --> 00:09:48.640
as an advocate leader
and coordinator

00:09:48.640 --> 00:09:52.300
for the creative arts
program here at MIT.

00:09:52.300 --> 00:09:53.140
Believe me.

00:09:53.140 --> 00:09:56.590
Ellen Harris serves
superbly in that role.

00:09:56.590 --> 00:09:59.319
Ladies and gentlemen,
professor Ellen Harris.

00:09:59.319 --> 00:10:02.672
[APPLAUSE]

00:10:10.350 --> 00:10:11.970
Thank you, Chuck.

00:10:11.970 --> 00:10:13.020
Good morning.

00:10:13.020 --> 00:10:14.460
Good morning.

00:10:14.460 --> 00:10:15.060
Oh, good.

00:10:18.580 --> 00:10:23.170
It's often been said that at MIT
one's education is like taking

00:10:23.170 --> 00:10:24.910
a drink from a firehose.

00:10:24.910 --> 00:10:29.690
What I have noticed is
that so is Tech Day.

00:10:29.690 --> 00:10:33.020
We have you here before 9:00
in the morning after having you

00:10:33.020 --> 00:10:35.930
out very late last
night, and I appreciate

00:10:35.930 --> 00:10:40.670
seeing you all here so
bright and raring to go.

00:10:40.670 --> 00:10:43.160
I'm proud and privileged
to welcome you

00:10:43.160 --> 00:10:47.360
to The Wonder of It All,
the Arts at MIT Technology

00:10:47.360 --> 00:10:49.970
Day 1994.

00:10:49.970 --> 00:10:53.180
And I want to add my
thanks to Jorge Rodriguez,

00:10:53.180 --> 00:10:55.940
the chair of the alumni
Tech Day committee.

00:10:55.940 --> 00:11:00.230
And also to Eliza Dame,
who does so much, more

00:11:00.230 --> 00:11:06.090
than I can possibly say, to make
Tech Day work year after year.

00:11:06.090 --> 00:11:09.750
Many people like to talk
about the arts at MIT

00:11:09.750 --> 00:11:13.170
as if it's an anomaly,
as if it's a surprise,

00:11:13.170 --> 00:11:15.390
or as if it's something new.

00:11:15.390 --> 00:11:18.180
But of course you know
better than anyone else

00:11:18.180 --> 00:11:20.290
that that is not the case.

00:11:20.290 --> 00:11:24.030
The arts have been a part
of MIT since its founding.

00:11:24.030 --> 00:11:28.020
MIT was the first school
to establish a school

00:11:28.020 --> 00:11:31.320
of architecture in 1868.

00:11:31.320 --> 00:11:34.710
It actually began offering
courses in architecture

00:11:34.710 --> 00:11:38.070
in its first year, 1865.

00:11:38.070 --> 00:11:40.830
In that same year we
were offering courses

00:11:40.830 --> 00:11:43.770
in photography and in drawing.

00:11:43.770 --> 00:11:47.550
In 1884 we not only
established the Banjo Club,

00:11:47.550 --> 00:11:50.640
but we established
the Glee Club.

00:11:50.640 --> 00:11:54.630
In 1928 we established
Drama Shop,

00:11:54.630 --> 00:11:58.320
the forerunner of our
theater curriculum today.

00:11:58.320 --> 00:12:03.060
And in 1948 John
Corley came to MIT

00:12:03.060 --> 00:12:07.470
to establish the MIT band, an
organization that he continues

00:12:07.470 --> 00:12:10.860
to conduct today.

00:12:10.860 --> 00:12:14.185
[APPLAUSE]

00:12:18.470 --> 00:12:21.170
In 1950 the Hayden
Gallery opened,

00:12:21.170 --> 00:12:24.560
which was the forerunner of our
now internationally renowned

00:12:24.560 --> 00:12:27.410
List Visual Arts Center.

00:12:27.410 --> 00:12:32.840
But perhaps most
importantly, in 1944--

00:12:32.840 --> 00:12:34.880
50 years ago today--

00:12:34.880 --> 00:12:41.080
MIT first established its
eight-semester sequence

00:12:41.080 --> 00:12:43.110
in humanities.

00:12:43.110 --> 00:12:47.220
Those of you who have been
at the Institute since 1944

00:12:47.220 --> 00:12:50.280
will remember your eight
courses in the humanities.

00:12:50.280 --> 00:12:52.330
They used to be different
than they are today.

00:12:52.330 --> 00:12:59.670
But in 1944 they included
I think a first-year course

00:12:59.670 --> 00:13:02.260
in writing and
English literature,

00:13:02.260 --> 00:13:06.150
second-year course in history,
third-year course where

00:13:06.150 --> 00:13:08.590
you could choose from
a number of subjects,

00:13:08.590 --> 00:13:10.200
including economics.

00:13:10.200 --> 00:13:13.300
And in the fourth year a
wider selection of courses,

00:13:13.300 --> 00:13:17.580
including a yearlong
course in music.

00:13:17.580 --> 00:13:20.490
Because of this
eight-semester sequence

00:13:20.490 --> 00:13:25.000
of courses, which parallels
the eight-semester courses

00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:32.300
in science, MIT has had for many
years a program in education

00:13:32.300 --> 00:13:35.500
which has been referred
to here as dual literacy.

00:13:35.500 --> 00:13:38.410
MIT students have been
asked to be literate

00:13:38.410 --> 00:13:42.730
not only in science, but in
humanities and in the arts.

00:13:42.730 --> 00:13:47.800
In fact, MIT may be one of the
few educational institutions

00:13:47.800 --> 00:13:51.700
in the country that asks
this of its students.

00:13:51.700 --> 00:13:54.580
It is very, very easy
at many liberal arts

00:13:54.580 --> 00:13:58.310
institutions to
avoid the sciences.

00:13:58.310 --> 00:14:02.040
Not so easy at MIT to
avoid the humanities.

00:14:05.540 --> 00:14:09.440
In fact, this dual
literacy has meant

00:14:09.440 --> 00:14:14.930
that we have within
our students and alums

00:14:14.930 --> 00:14:18.830
many people who have gone
on to careers in the arts.

00:14:18.830 --> 00:14:23.810
In fact, in the class of 1994,
I can think of four cases

00:14:23.810 --> 00:14:25.740
immediately.

00:14:25.740 --> 00:14:28.110
Two young men who
have double majored

00:14:28.110 --> 00:14:31.170
in mathematics and
music, both of whom

00:14:31.170 --> 00:14:33.630
are going on right now
to graduate school.

00:14:33.630 --> 00:14:37.440
One to the University of
Chicago, and one to Duke

00:14:37.440 --> 00:14:40.480
in music composition.

00:14:40.480 --> 00:14:42.430
We have another
student who majored

00:14:42.430 --> 00:14:46.240
in civil engineering
who has received

00:14:46.240 --> 00:14:52.070
a full scholarship to pursue
writing at Boston University.

00:14:52.070 --> 00:14:55.820
And we have another student
who majored in theater

00:14:55.820 --> 00:15:01.350
at MIT, who is going on to
pursue his work in theater, who

00:15:01.350 --> 00:15:03.150
hopes to establish
his own theater

00:15:03.150 --> 00:15:05.955
company in the
countryside of Mexico.

00:15:08.690 --> 00:15:10.640
I sometimes think
we must graduate

00:15:10.640 --> 00:15:13.340
the most scientifically
literate artists in the world.

00:15:16.250 --> 00:15:18.610
But this is not something
that was just true

00:15:18.610 --> 00:15:22.720
of the class of 1994, and I was
looking at the reunion classes

00:15:22.720 --> 00:15:24.850
for some other examples.

00:15:24.850 --> 00:15:27.880
And obviously, we have
a lot of people today

00:15:27.880 --> 00:15:31.400
here from the class of 1944.

00:15:31.400 --> 00:15:32.278
Right?

00:15:32.278 --> 00:15:35.694
[APPLAUSE]

00:15:36.670 --> 00:15:39.110
Well, one of your
members, John Bavicchi,

00:15:39.110 --> 00:15:41.980
who graduated with a
major in management,

00:15:41.980 --> 00:15:44.650
has gone on to be a
very well-known composer

00:15:44.650 --> 00:15:47.230
and conductor, who has
been for many years

00:15:47.230 --> 00:15:50.260
on the faculty of the
Berklee School of Music.

00:15:50.260 --> 00:15:53.040
From the class of '64--

00:15:53.040 --> 00:15:54.850
'64?

00:15:54.850 --> 00:15:56.360
Do I hear the class of '64?--

00:16:00.120 --> 00:16:02.470
we have John Miller
who double majored

00:16:02.470 --> 00:16:04.720
in humanities and
engineering, who

00:16:04.720 --> 00:16:07.660
has made his career as
principal bassoonist

00:16:07.660 --> 00:16:10.410
with the Minnesota Orchestra.

00:16:10.410 --> 00:16:12.259
Class of '69.

00:16:12.259 --> 00:16:15.253
[APPLAUSE]

00:16:16.750 --> 00:16:20.020
You had with you for
some time James Woods,

00:16:20.020 --> 00:16:21.820
who was majoring in
political science

00:16:21.820 --> 00:16:25.600
before he went on to become
a very well-known actor.

00:16:25.600 --> 00:16:27.364
In '79--

00:16:27.364 --> 00:16:30.208
[SCATTERED APPLAUSE]

00:16:32.110 --> 00:16:33.700
I think we need
more energy folks.

00:16:37.710 --> 00:16:42.240
Liz Maruska, who went on to
become a practicing artist

00:16:42.240 --> 00:16:45.180
on the West Coast, who
paints beautiful canvases

00:16:45.180 --> 00:16:50.080
in watercolor and gouache
expressing time and space.

00:16:50.080 --> 00:16:52.905
And finally, class
of '89, five years.

00:16:52.905 --> 00:16:54.938
[APPLAUSE]

00:16:56.250 --> 00:17:00.270
Ken Goodson, who majored in
both mechanical engineering

00:17:00.270 --> 00:17:02.670
and music, received
his master of science

00:17:02.670 --> 00:17:07.079
in '91 in mechanical
engineering, his PhD in '93,

00:17:07.079 --> 00:17:10.109
is now studying and
performing as a baritone

00:17:10.109 --> 00:17:13.490
soloist in Germany.

00:17:13.490 --> 00:17:19.250
I love, about Ken, that when
he performed at Tanglewood

00:17:19.250 --> 00:17:22.160
as an MIT grad, it is
only appropriate that when

00:17:22.160 --> 00:17:25.730
singing in Stravinsky's
work, "The Flood,"

00:17:25.730 --> 00:17:28.498
Ken Goodson play
the role of God.

00:17:28.498 --> 00:17:30.938
[LAUGHTER]

00:17:34.360 --> 00:17:38.200
But obviously MIT does not
graduate all of its students

00:17:38.200 --> 00:17:41.890
to go on in the arts.

00:17:41.890 --> 00:17:43.990
You don't need to be
an arts professional

00:17:43.990 --> 00:17:46.120
to benefit from the arts.

00:17:46.120 --> 00:17:49.060
And whether you worked in Doc
Edgerton's strobe photography

00:17:49.060 --> 00:17:52.660
lab, or perhaps
became interested

00:17:52.660 --> 00:17:54.430
in physics through
Bernice Abbott's

00:17:54.430 --> 00:17:57.370
extraordinary photographs
of physical phenomena that

00:17:57.370 --> 00:17:59.890
were published in many
high school textbooks

00:17:59.890 --> 00:18:03.070
and that were taking
at MIT, or whether you

00:18:03.070 --> 00:18:05.860
played in the symphony,
or sang in the Glee Club,

00:18:05.860 --> 00:18:08.850
or acted in a
theatrical production,

00:18:08.850 --> 00:18:12.730
I am sure that at some point
art touched your life at MIT,

00:18:12.730 --> 00:18:15.850
and has continued to touch
your life thereafter.

00:18:15.850 --> 00:18:18.280
Arts offer us discipline.

00:18:18.280 --> 00:18:22.210
Anyone who has practiced
the piano knows that.

00:18:22.210 --> 00:18:25.700
Arts offer us imagination.

00:18:25.700 --> 00:18:29.320
They offer us the
flexibility of a pursuit

00:18:29.320 --> 00:18:32.150
where there is not
one right answer.

00:18:32.150 --> 00:18:35.230
But they also offer us
new ways of organization--

00:18:35.230 --> 00:18:40.090
of organization of sound,
and space, and time.

00:18:40.090 --> 00:18:44.800
The arts offer us a doorway into
cultures other than our own,

00:18:44.800 --> 00:18:48.250
that enables us not only
to tolerate but respect

00:18:48.250 --> 00:18:49.930
difference.

00:18:49.930 --> 00:18:51.860
The arts challenge
us, surprise us.

00:18:51.860 --> 00:18:54.510
Sometimes they anger us.

00:18:54.510 --> 00:18:58.170
But the arts also bring
us wonder and joy.

00:18:58.170 --> 00:19:01.650
And today we hope to explore,
through our presentations,

00:19:01.650 --> 00:19:05.090
the wonder and joy of
the arts today at MIT.

00:19:07.930 --> 00:19:10.390
This of course,
leads me directly

00:19:10.390 --> 00:19:14.560
to our first speaker, Philip
Morrison, Institute Professor

00:19:14.560 --> 00:19:16.960
emeritus at MIT.

00:19:16.960 --> 00:19:19.330
You are probably
familiar with his work

00:19:19.330 --> 00:19:21.260
in astrophysics and cosmology.

00:19:21.260 --> 00:19:25.880
Maybe less familiar with
his work as a filmmaker.

00:19:25.880 --> 00:19:29.660
I first came to know
Philip through a copy

00:19:29.660 --> 00:19:32.360
of a keynote
address that he gave

00:19:32.360 --> 00:19:37.790
in 1986 for the 15th anniversary
of the Council for the Arts

00:19:37.790 --> 00:19:42.020
at MIT, an organization
founded in 1971

00:19:42.020 --> 00:19:48.020
to advise and support MIT in the
expansion of its arts programs.

00:19:48.020 --> 00:19:53.060
At that time he
said, and I quote,

00:19:53.060 --> 00:19:57.170
"It's clear that if an
institution like this one which

00:19:57.170 --> 00:20:02.510
deals with the material world
did not have the side of art,

00:20:02.510 --> 00:20:05.720
it would be a self-defeating
proposition which in the end

00:20:05.720 --> 00:20:10.910
would be all genetic seed
and no fruit to the apple.

00:20:10.910 --> 00:20:15.080
And an apple is
not made that way."

00:20:15.080 --> 00:20:19.160
I think that I can do no
better in introducing Professor

00:20:19.160 --> 00:20:23.510
Morrison than to quote his
colleague Victor Weisskopf who

00:20:23.510 --> 00:20:27.380
said of him, "Scientific
knowledge and understanding is

00:20:27.380 --> 00:20:30.170
not a purely cerebral affair.

00:20:30.170 --> 00:20:33.050
It is soaked with
emotion, excitement,

00:20:33.050 --> 00:20:35.450
and nervous tension,
as everybody

00:20:35.450 --> 00:20:39.740
knows who has ever heard
Professor Morrison speak."

00:20:39.740 --> 00:20:41.240
Ladies and gentlemen,
please help me

00:20:41.240 --> 00:20:43.160
to welcome Professor Morrison.

00:20:43.160 --> 00:20:46.618
[APPLAUSE]

00:21:19.290 --> 00:21:20.130
Thank you very much.

00:21:26.520 --> 00:21:27.450
Much equipment here.

00:21:27.450 --> 00:21:30.150
All of it seems to work.

00:21:30.150 --> 00:21:33.060
I'm especially impressed by
the video projection system.

00:21:37.450 --> 00:21:40.900
The Wonder of it All
at MIT: The Arts at MIT

00:21:40.900 --> 00:21:42.430
is a very nice title.

00:21:42.430 --> 00:21:43.290
Doesn't say much.

00:21:43.290 --> 00:21:45.430
Commits very few speakers.

00:21:45.430 --> 00:21:48.880
I would certainly like to
affix firmly onto the word

00:21:48.880 --> 00:21:53.560
"wonder," because clearly
that's the kind of motive which

00:21:53.560 --> 00:21:57.430
lies behind nearly all the
things that go on at MIT,

00:21:57.430 --> 00:21:58.990
translated as
eventually they are

00:21:58.990 --> 00:22:02.080
into the hard tasks
of earning a living

00:22:02.080 --> 00:22:03.280
and making this world work.

00:22:07.390 --> 00:22:09.220
The old classical
Chinese scholars

00:22:09.220 --> 00:22:12.970
always like to pretend that the
ideas they had were very old.

00:22:12.970 --> 00:22:14.529
So they found some
mysterious figure

00:22:14.529 --> 00:22:16.570
of the past who nobody
knew much about, typically

00:22:16.570 --> 00:22:17.641
the Yellow Emperor.

00:22:17.641 --> 00:22:20.140
And they said, well, the Yellow
Emperor first said, and then

00:22:20.140 --> 00:22:21.730
they made up their own books.

00:22:21.730 --> 00:22:23.285
[LAUGHTER]

00:22:23.285 --> 00:22:24.160
We don't do that now.

00:22:24.160 --> 00:22:25.701
We have a much more
forward-looking--

00:22:25.701 --> 00:22:27.110
we don't look back in general.

00:22:27.110 --> 00:22:28.510
It's always new.

00:22:28.510 --> 00:22:31.060
That's the same
error in reverse.

00:22:31.060 --> 00:22:34.720
People have this tendency
for bipolar positioning,

00:22:34.720 --> 00:22:37.030
bipolar stability of large axis.

00:22:37.030 --> 00:22:38.290
Because everything is both--

00:22:38.290 --> 00:22:40.210
has something old,
has something new,

00:22:40.210 --> 00:22:42.130
or it's not being
pursued very far.

00:22:42.130 --> 00:22:43.870
That's the typical lesson.

00:22:43.870 --> 00:22:46.300
Ask a historian, he'll
take it back for you.

00:22:46.300 --> 00:22:50.320
Ask a projector of the
future, he'll push it forward.

00:22:50.320 --> 00:22:51.850
Both things lie in time.

00:22:54.740 --> 00:22:57.900
I wondered however, and I
didn't always know this,

00:22:57.900 --> 00:23:00.046
and I became more and
more convinced of it

00:23:00.046 --> 00:23:05.510
in the past recent
times, few years maybe,

00:23:05.510 --> 00:23:12.740
the proposition that this
issue that we now debate

00:23:12.740 --> 00:23:15.410
is extraordinarily old.

00:23:15.410 --> 00:23:18.080
I could invoke no
newer phrase for it

00:23:18.080 --> 00:23:26.630
than this Latin tag of the
Institute, mens et manus, mind

00:23:26.630 --> 00:23:29.000
and hand, to talk about it.

00:23:29.000 --> 00:23:31.970
Because of course, that
is the exquisite pleasure

00:23:31.970 --> 00:23:33.860
of the MIT ambience--

00:23:33.860 --> 00:23:38.270
that minds and hands are
equally well-occupied.

00:23:38.270 --> 00:23:43.120
That means to say that a
lot of mechanical things

00:23:43.120 --> 00:23:45.440
are done-- a lot of things
in the material world,

00:23:45.440 --> 00:23:47.960
or with the refractory
symbols of the world,

00:23:47.960 --> 00:23:50.570
they're hammered away
at, whether it be metal,

00:23:50.570 --> 00:23:52.470
or melodies, or whatever it is.

00:23:52.470 --> 00:23:55.130
But it's not done by
and large by rote.

00:23:55.130 --> 00:23:57.410
Obviously some rote goes
into all operations.

00:23:57.410 --> 00:23:59.690
Experts become routinized.

00:23:59.690 --> 00:24:03.020
But thought is at the basis.

00:24:03.020 --> 00:24:06.530
And it turns out that
that unity, which

00:24:06.530 --> 00:24:15.330
is a profound unity between
art and the triune quality

00:24:15.330 --> 00:24:20.280
of modifying and knowing
the world physically, craft,

00:24:20.280 --> 00:24:23.580
engineering, and science
in their relationships

00:24:23.580 --> 00:24:25.310
goes back a very long time.

00:24:25.310 --> 00:24:28.840
And I brought along a few
slides to justify that

00:24:28.840 --> 00:24:30.420
and to get us well started.

00:24:30.420 --> 00:24:33.120
So it's not a new task
that we're looking into.

00:24:33.120 --> 00:24:36.640
This wonderful picture in the
center, collection of rocks,

00:24:36.640 --> 00:24:38.850
deserves some close attention.

00:24:38.850 --> 00:24:40.680
Those rocks are
not natural rocks.

00:24:40.680 --> 00:24:42.600
The fact that they are
quite similar I think

00:24:42.600 --> 00:24:44.040
is pretty evident.

00:24:44.040 --> 00:24:46.350
It's also quite evident--

00:24:46.350 --> 00:24:49.050
if you look more closely,
not easy to see perhaps,

00:24:49.050 --> 00:24:50.920
it's better to handle them.

00:24:50.920 --> 00:24:53.340
We can't do that, but I think
it's enough to suggest it

00:24:53.340 --> 00:24:55.410
in a context like this.

00:24:55.410 --> 00:24:59.540
You can see the three edges
here, the double ones there.

00:24:59.540 --> 00:25:03.260
Every one has faceted edges.

00:25:03.260 --> 00:25:06.170
This one has in fact a
slight double, a kind

00:25:06.170 --> 00:25:08.000
of blade-like edge and so on.

00:25:08.000 --> 00:25:09.320
This is a pile of rocks--

00:25:09.320 --> 00:25:12.770
a section, a collection--
made by the excavators

00:25:12.770 --> 00:25:16.250
in Olduvai Gorge in the
rift of East Africa.

00:25:16.250 --> 00:25:17.600
These were not found that way.

00:25:17.600 --> 00:25:21.080
They were found stratum after
stratum, carefully located,

00:25:21.080 --> 00:25:23.900
carefully arranged in position
so they could be dated.

00:25:23.900 --> 00:25:26.150
But the photographer simply
gathered a lot of them

00:25:26.150 --> 00:25:27.650
to give us an
impression of how many

00:25:27.650 --> 00:25:33.870
hundreds, indeed many thousands,
of these stone tools are found.

00:25:33.870 --> 00:25:36.440
Now, the remarkable part
about them-- this of course

00:25:36.440 --> 00:25:38.630
is mens et manus.

00:25:38.630 --> 00:25:41.120
Mens is pretty thin still.

00:25:41.120 --> 00:25:44.040
These are not even
our own species.

00:25:44.040 --> 00:25:46.010
These are our distant forebears.

00:25:46.010 --> 00:25:47.930
They were poor.

00:25:47.930 --> 00:25:51.890
They first walked the earth
fearful of the beasts.

00:25:51.890 --> 00:25:54.410
Pretty soon that
stopped, and the beasts

00:25:54.410 --> 00:25:55.940
tended to avoid them.

00:25:55.940 --> 00:25:57.770
Before that perhaps
even they scavenged

00:25:57.770 --> 00:25:59.600
on the kill of the lion.

00:25:59.600 --> 00:26:05.970
But sooner or later, this kind
of operation symbolized here--

00:26:05.970 --> 00:26:09.830
mind and hand-- began to
change where they were.

00:26:09.830 --> 00:26:13.970
And they did marvelous things
on a very slow time scale.

00:26:13.970 --> 00:26:16.850
The pieces represented
in this collection,

00:26:16.850 --> 00:26:20.000
just that arbitrary piece
of a large collection,

00:26:20.000 --> 00:26:25.490
are measured not by centuries,
but by thousands of centuries.

00:26:25.490 --> 00:26:26.030
Yes.

00:26:26.030 --> 00:26:27.620
By hundreds of
thousands of years.

00:26:27.620 --> 00:26:31.430
A few 100,000 years corresponds
to a typical collection

00:26:31.430 --> 00:26:34.590
at Olduvai, which might extend
to a million years or more.

00:26:34.590 --> 00:26:39.410
But to be generous we'll say
a few thousand centuries.

00:26:39.410 --> 00:26:41.780
Now the striking part of
this is that these tools,

00:26:41.780 --> 00:26:43.970
without very much
change, are found

00:26:43.970 --> 00:26:45.770
over a very large
part of the world

00:26:45.770 --> 00:26:48.710
as the next slide will show.

00:26:48.710 --> 00:26:49.340
There it is.

00:26:49.340 --> 00:26:50.360
Here are famous sites.

00:26:50.360 --> 00:26:53.300
Anyone who has read anything
about early archeology

00:26:53.300 --> 00:26:57.120
will recognize these
kinds of locations.

00:26:57.120 --> 00:26:59.670
From Britain up here,
into Europe, France,

00:26:59.670 --> 00:27:00.780
at the Pyrenees.

00:27:00.780 --> 00:27:02.970
Especially very
important in there.

00:27:02.970 --> 00:27:04.920
All across Africa, East Africa.

00:27:04.920 --> 00:27:08.940
Here we have Olduvai itself
and down to Cape Town.

00:27:08.940 --> 00:27:12.660
So from Britain to Cape Town,
and from Cape Town across Asia,

00:27:12.660 --> 00:27:19.330
Central Asia, India, North
China, Peking, further north

00:27:19.330 --> 00:27:22.930
still and into Java.

00:27:22.930 --> 00:27:24.760
All these-- the major sites.

00:27:24.760 --> 00:27:28.180
Each site feathers out into
many, many little excavations.

00:27:28.180 --> 00:27:31.270
And from all of these places
about the same kind of tools

00:27:31.270 --> 00:27:32.086
are collected.

00:27:32.086 --> 00:27:34.210
Yes, the experts argue a
bit, and they can somewhat

00:27:34.210 --> 00:27:34.955
classify them.

00:27:34.955 --> 00:27:36.580
But it's really quite
hard to date them

00:27:36.580 --> 00:27:40.870
except by the attendant
pieces of mineral which

00:27:40.870 --> 00:27:43.270
can enable you to date
them by radioactive means.

00:27:43.270 --> 00:27:46.360
But you'll notice that
not one site is found

00:27:46.360 --> 00:27:50.210
in these islands or Australia.

00:27:50.210 --> 00:27:51.760
And I've left out the Americas.

00:27:51.760 --> 00:27:53.290
No use to put it on the map.

00:27:53.290 --> 00:27:55.870
Because not one
site of this kind

00:27:55.870 --> 00:27:58.930
has ever surely been
found in America.

00:27:58.930 --> 00:27:59.580
No.

00:27:59.580 --> 00:28:02.890
Australia, the great
islands near Australia,

00:28:02.890 --> 00:28:07.150
and the two Americas do not
belong to these people, who

00:28:07.150 --> 00:28:09.520
I don't wish to say are--

00:28:09.520 --> 00:28:10.690
they are our ancestors.

00:28:10.690 --> 00:28:12.460
Cannot speak ill of them.

00:28:12.460 --> 00:28:15.730
They were in fact
the Prometheans.

00:28:15.730 --> 00:28:18.250
Because somewhere in
the middle of their span

00:28:18.250 --> 00:28:23.210
Homo erectus figured out how
to make and control fire.

00:28:23.210 --> 00:28:25.820
And the hammer at
MIT is entirely

00:28:25.820 --> 00:28:29.150
secondary to the
fire, and all that

00:28:29.150 --> 00:28:30.317
goes with that symbolically.

00:28:30.317 --> 00:28:32.608
So I don't show you those
sites, though of course there

00:28:32.608 --> 00:28:34.130
are very many sites in America.

00:28:34.130 --> 00:28:37.400
But none of them show
the bones or the tools

00:28:37.400 --> 00:28:39.110
of these ancient folk.

00:28:39.110 --> 00:28:43.280
We truly, like the Australians--
the Australian Aboriginals,

00:28:43.280 --> 00:28:47.990
the Native Americans--
are the modern humans,

00:28:47.990 --> 00:28:49.970
like all the rest of
this place has also been

00:28:49.970 --> 00:28:51.230
filled in by modern humans.

00:28:51.230 --> 00:28:52.430
I don't mean to say we're--

00:28:52.430 --> 00:28:53.420
of course we're not.

00:28:53.420 --> 00:28:55.860
Most modern humans live
in this part of the world.

00:28:55.860 --> 00:28:58.850
But the same species
occupies this whole region.

00:28:58.850 --> 00:29:03.170
And only after a long time,
after thousands of centuries,

00:29:03.170 --> 00:29:07.470
it managed, they managed, in the
course of some internal change,

00:29:07.470 --> 00:29:09.440
which is what we would
call a change in species

00:29:09.440 --> 00:29:11.900
to Homo sapiens, they managed--

00:29:11.900 --> 00:29:14.120
by means that can be well
debated and interesting

00:29:14.120 --> 00:29:15.220
to study--

00:29:15.220 --> 00:29:17.900
they managed to be able
to cross the then much

00:29:17.900 --> 00:29:21.800
shallower coastal seas,
and finally the great Gulf

00:29:21.800 --> 00:29:23.120
of the Timor Trench.

00:29:23.120 --> 00:29:26.450
And come somehow by canoe
or raft to Australia,

00:29:26.450 --> 00:29:29.870
and in the same way go
across the relenting Arctic

00:29:29.870 --> 00:29:31.880
where the glaciers were
melting, and follow

00:29:31.880 --> 00:29:33.300
the coast line of America.

00:29:33.300 --> 00:29:36.830
And go down the full length of
both continents rather quickly,

00:29:36.830 --> 00:29:39.560
and that completed the
occupation of the world

00:29:39.560 --> 00:29:42.000
by modern humans.

00:29:42.000 --> 00:29:43.250
So we are the new world.

00:29:43.250 --> 00:29:45.170
Not because of
Columbus or any feature

00:29:45.170 --> 00:29:46.310
of historical importance.

00:29:46.310 --> 00:29:47.630
Those are small matters.

00:29:47.630 --> 00:29:49.670
The real thing was
we we were new,

00:29:49.670 --> 00:29:52.460
because the new people who
filled up the old lands very

00:29:52.460 --> 00:29:55.670
well had a way also
to get to new lands

00:29:55.670 --> 00:29:57.080
never before occupied.

00:29:57.080 --> 00:30:02.600
So our histories go back only
a few hundred centuries, not

00:30:02.600 --> 00:30:03.844
a few thousand centuries.

00:30:03.844 --> 00:30:05.510
There's an order of
magnitude difference

00:30:05.510 --> 00:30:10.770
between the kind of thing you
saw in the Olduvai rocks--

00:30:10.770 --> 00:30:14.020
bifaces, choppers, hand
axes they're called,

00:30:14.020 --> 00:30:17.840
and the kind of thing that
came when a new set of persons

00:30:17.840 --> 00:30:20.840
of whom we are all
descended, we can prove

00:30:20.840 --> 00:30:25.230
by the deepest of
biological means

00:30:25.230 --> 00:30:30.050
how united the whole world
is in our single species,

00:30:30.050 --> 00:30:33.630
with subspecies as we well know.

00:30:33.630 --> 00:30:35.400
And the next slide
will show what

00:30:35.400 --> 00:30:37.950
you find in these new sites.

00:30:37.950 --> 00:30:41.640
Now please compare these,
which are nice American pieces.

00:30:41.640 --> 00:30:46.720
They were made about
12,000 or 14,000 years ago.

00:30:46.720 --> 00:30:49.680
These are found in
a region in Montana.

00:30:49.680 --> 00:30:53.250
And of course they're
specialized for killing

00:30:53.250 --> 00:30:54.329
big game.

00:30:54.329 --> 00:30:56.370
And if you'd like to know
what the big game was--

00:30:56.370 --> 00:30:57.500
probably you do know--

00:30:57.500 --> 00:30:59.580
it was elephants.

00:30:59.580 --> 00:31:04.420
Pieces quite like this,
spear points like this,

00:31:04.420 --> 00:31:10.210
have been found in contact with
the bones of bison and mammoth.

00:31:10.210 --> 00:31:14.350
And of course, where the
other rocks had 10 or 20

00:31:14.350 --> 00:31:17.110
rather cunning blows struck
which you and I could not do,

00:31:17.110 --> 00:31:20.140
which people can train
themselves to do today,

00:31:20.140 --> 00:31:24.880
these have many hundreds
of knowing strokes

00:31:24.880 --> 00:31:26.392
to chip the blade
just correctly.

00:31:26.392 --> 00:31:27.850
And even along the
edge you can see

00:31:27.850 --> 00:31:31.480
to sharpen it with a wonderful
thing, very detailed marks

00:31:31.480 --> 00:31:33.700
up there, all of which are
significant in producing

00:31:33.700 --> 00:31:35.890
a handy and useful blade.

00:31:35.890 --> 00:31:40.120
So I have no doubt to say this
is craftsmanship, possibly even

00:31:40.120 --> 00:31:40.720
engineering.

00:31:40.720 --> 00:31:42.820
At least its high-quality
craftsmanship,

00:31:42.820 --> 00:31:45.610
because we know now that many
of these blades have to be heat

00:31:45.610 --> 00:31:47.920
treated in order to allow
the chert and the flint

00:31:47.920 --> 00:31:49.425
to respond to the chipping.

00:31:49.425 --> 00:31:52.050
But now I'd like to go a little
farther and show what they did.

00:31:52.050 --> 00:31:54.050
And it happens that the
preservations I have now

00:31:54.050 --> 00:31:56.080
are again the same new
people, our people,

00:31:56.080 --> 00:31:58.090
but now coming
from France and not

00:31:58.090 --> 00:32:01.380
from Montana, the following.

00:32:01.380 --> 00:32:05.160
A bone object used
for working sinew

00:32:05.160 --> 00:32:07.040
and straightening arrow shafts.

00:32:07.040 --> 00:32:08.340
You can see the hole there.

00:32:08.340 --> 00:32:11.130
But it wasn't enough
to make it utilitarian.

00:32:11.130 --> 00:32:13.770
It has this marvelous
leaping horse

00:32:13.770 --> 00:32:16.410
worked into the same material.

00:32:16.410 --> 00:32:24.630
And this was about 200 or 250
centuries old from Bruniquel,

00:32:24.630 --> 00:32:26.120
a cave in France.

00:32:26.120 --> 00:32:27.830
We have art so much
from France and Spain

00:32:27.830 --> 00:32:30.500
because the caverns are
so well-protected there.

00:32:30.500 --> 00:32:32.660
They guarded the art
behind stalactites

00:32:32.660 --> 00:32:34.710
for many thousands of years.

00:32:34.710 --> 00:32:36.560
And the next slide,
perhaps even more

00:32:36.560 --> 00:32:42.120
striking, an image itself,
an elk of the period.

00:32:42.120 --> 00:32:45.200
The biologist will find
fossils that fit it quite well.

00:32:45.200 --> 00:32:49.610
Beautifully drawn in
charcoal and ocher

00:32:49.610 --> 00:32:51.080
on the wall of the French cave.

00:32:51.080 --> 00:32:52.995
This was the famous
cave of Lascaux.

00:32:55.780 --> 00:32:59.820
So I rest the case that
the relationship that

00:32:59.820 --> 00:33:01.990
changed, that came
to human beings,

00:33:01.990 --> 00:33:03.640
we don't exactly
know what it is.

00:33:03.640 --> 00:33:05.440
Most likely it was
the acquisition

00:33:05.440 --> 00:33:07.230
of a powerful
language, which enabled

00:33:07.230 --> 00:33:09.910
them to work cooperatively
and to model the future,

00:33:09.910 --> 00:33:12.940
gave rise to the
craft and the art,

00:33:12.940 --> 00:33:15.220
which you unmistakably
see here, which goes back

00:33:15.220 --> 00:33:20.350
not merely thousands of years,
but tens of thousands of years.

00:33:20.350 --> 00:33:22.990
This particular piece is dated
about 15,000 or 18,000 years

00:33:22.990 --> 00:33:26.210
ago, the previous one
about 20,000 years ago.

00:33:26.210 --> 00:33:29.710
And all from that
same flood of persons

00:33:29.710 --> 00:33:31.851
who occupy the
world, maybe in all

00:33:31.851 --> 00:33:33.850
not more than half a
million of them by the time

00:33:33.850 --> 00:33:35.290
the Americas were settled.

00:33:35.290 --> 00:33:40.360
And we've grown them from that
half million to half trillion,

00:33:40.360 --> 00:33:45.435
a very large number of
powers of 10 in population.

00:33:48.830 --> 00:33:50.030
Thank you.

00:33:50.030 --> 00:33:53.930
Now the linkage
between the craft--

00:33:53.930 --> 00:33:58.730
engineering, science,
art-- between hand and mind

00:33:58.730 --> 00:34:01.100
expressed in this
way, I maintain

00:34:01.100 --> 00:34:04.430
this is demonstrably old.

00:34:04.430 --> 00:34:07.140
Indeed, we can see it even
in the beginnings of science,

00:34:07.140 --> 00:34:10.429
the time of the Enlightenment
of the late Renaissance,

00:34:10.429 --> 00:34:12.199
you couldn't buy
a microscope that

00:34:12.199 --> 00:34:15.860
wasn't worked up and chased
and engraved to bear some art.

00:34:15.860 --> 00:34:16.940
Partly that was style.

00:34:16.940 --> 00:34:17.917
I'm not denying it.

00:34:17.917 --> 00:34:19.250
Partly that was royal patronage.

00:34:19.250 --> 00:34:20.719
I'm not denying that either.

00:34:20.719 --> 00:34:24.199
Nevertheless, the work of
the hand was to be admired.

00:34:24.199 --> 00:34:27.080
And it was rare to have
such skilled craftsmanship

00:34:27.080 --> 00:34:29.659
in these new materials
with a new purpose.

00:34:29.659 --> 00:34:32.810
And to some extent, we've
lost that feeling because

00:34:32.810 --> 00:34:36.679
of a utilitarian quality,
and the already great expense

00:34:36.679 --> 00:34:39.050
of making the complex
instruments which

00:34:39.050 --> 00:34:43.909
now replace the sledgehammer
of the MIT seal.

00:34:43.909 --> 00:34:45.679
Or augment the sledgehammer.

00:34:45.679 --> 00:34:47.429
I don't want to run
down the sledgehammer.

00:34:47.429 --> 00:34:50.235
Still a very
indispensable instrument.

00:34:50.235 --> 00:34:51.860
What I would like to
emphasize, though,

00:34:51.860 --> 00:34:57.980
is that this connection
is a profound connection.

00:34:57.980 --> 00:35:00.410
And I'd like to describe a
little bit why I think it's so,

00:35:00.410 --> 00:35:04.910
and why it parts the
hand-mind relationship

00:35:04.910 --> 00:35:10.979
from a more symbolic one, which
is not absent from either.

00:35:10.979 --> 00:35:12.770
Any of you who have
tried to learn a craft,

00:35:12.770 --> 00:35:15.350
or a skill, or above
all an art, knows

00:35:15.350 --> 00:35:18.260
how very close the reaction
of the hand and the mind

00:35:18.260 --> 00:35:20.960
must be to make anything
work the way you choose.

00:35:20.960 --> 00:35:25.830
And if you don't do that,
another draft has to be made.

00:35:25.830 --> 00:35:28.670
Now of course those
who deal in symbols

00:35:28.670 --> 00:35:31.460
alone have some of
the same quality,

00:35:31.460 --> 00:35:34.310
but the hand is
much less present.

00:35:34.310 --> 00:35:37.370
The symbols are, by
nature, more conventional.

00:35:37.370 --> 00:35:40.760
What is characteristic of
art and science engineering

00:35:40.760 --> 00:35:43.970
together is they represent--

00:35:43.970 --> 00:35:45.860
almost always they're
presented to us,

00:35:45.860 --> 00:35:49.490
and they need to be presented
to us in concrete form.

00:35:49.490 --> 00:35:51.830
In single concrete examples.

00:35:51.830 --> 00:35:54.020
Perhaps something more
than the poet Blake

00:35:54.020 --> 00:35:56.480
called "minute
particulars," which

00:35:56.480 --> 00:35:59.690
he felt all art and
science were compacted.

00:35:59.690 --> 00:36:01.940
But certainly not generalities.

00:36:01.940 --> 00:36:04.830
Generalities edged towards
philosophical description.

00:36:04.830 --> 00:36:06.512
True, there's a big
domain in-between.

00:36:06.512 --> 00:36:07.970
There are theorems
and propositions

00:36:07.970 --> 00:36:10.110
that are indispensable,
that come out of thought,

00:36:10.110 --> 00:36:12.270
and which govern many
of the things we do.

00:36:12.270 --> 00:36:14.660
But I think it's fair to
say that works of art,

00:36:14.660 --> 00:36:18.290
and the data of science,
and works of the engineer,

00:36:18.290 --> 00:36:20.040
are all concrete examples.

00:36:20.040 --> 00:36:24.050
And if you don't have those, you
have a very dry subject indeed,

00:36:24.050 --> 00:36:26.090
that consists of
nothing of theorems,

00:36:26.090 --> 00:36:29.477
about mechanics without notions,
or diagrams, or examples,

00:36:29.477 --> 00:36:32.060
or particulars, which I suffered
through as a graduate student

00:36:32.060 --> 00:36:35.990
once long ago at
another university

00:36:35.990 --> 00:36:37.820
when I was studying mechanics.

00:36:37.820 --> 00:36:39.410
We couldn't ask
questions, and we also

00:36:39.410 --> 00:36:41.000
couldn't understand
the propositions.

00:36:41.000 --> 00:36:45.160
We sure could learn
the words quite well.

00:36:45.160 --> 00:36:49.735
Now there are
differences, of course.

00:36:54.880 --> 00:36:57.970
I think that the principal
characteristic of art,

00:36:57.970 --> 00:37:00.497
which I admit it is
somewhat rapidly losing,

00:37:00.497 --> 00:37:02.830
but hasn't lost yet-- it can
be seen in almost all parts

00:37:02.830 --> 00:37:03.640
of it--

00:37:03.640 --> 00:37:06.790
its products, its
particulars, are

00:37:06.790 --> 00:37:09.550
perceptual to the human being.

00:37:09.550 --> 00:37:13.630
It's very hard to imagine
art translated through--

00:37:13.630 --> 00:37:15.460
it's not impossible,
we see it today--

00:37:15.460 --> 00:37:19.570
translated through some
intermediate device,

00:37:19.570 --> 00:37:21.840
which turns it into
printouts and numbers.

00:37:21.840 --> 00:37:23.010
Then you have to regraph it.

00:37:23.010 --> 00:37:23.920
This is hardly art.

00:37:23.920 --> 00:37:25.510
The immediacy is gone.

00:37:25.510 --> 00:37:27.850
Though of course, a great
deal of the world of science,

00:37:27.850 --> 00:37:29.410
great deal of the
data of science,

00:37:29.410 --> 00:37:32.440
go beyond human perception,
involve intermediation

00:37:32.440 --> 00:37:34.780
by instrumentation
of complicated kinds.

00:37:34.780 --> 00:37:37.447
And auxiliary calculations
to bring together

00:37:37.447 --> 00:37:40.030
the reading of the instruments
or program inside the computer,

00:37:40.030 --> 00:37:41.810
which is doing the same thing.

00:37:41.810 --> 00:37:43.810
And by and large, that's
the biggest distinction

00:37:43.810 --> 00:37:48.130
I can see on the side
of how the work is done.

00:37:48.130 --> 00:37:52.180
It is also true that
they are united in this.

00:37:52.180 --> 00:37:55.180
Niels Bohr defined
science not only as effort

00:37:55.180 --> 00:37:57.910
to order the world, which
surely it is, but there it

00:37:57.910 --> 00:37:58.840
does not deal--

00:37:58.840 --> 00:38:00.730
it does not differ
from philosophy,

00:38:00.730 --> 00:38:03.370
or for that matter from poetry.

00:38:03.370 --> 00:38:05.901
But it seeks to bring a
new experience, which again

00:38:05.901 --> 00:38:07.900
is close to art, though
perhaps rather different

00:38:07.900 --> 00:38:09.670
from the philosopher's work.

00:38:09.670 --> 00:38:11.650
And if we talk
about engineering,

00:38:11.650 --> 00:38:14.410
I think we have to say to bring
new means for the game you've

00:38:14.410 --> 00:38:16.600
experienced this,
too, is necessary.

00:38:16.600 --> 00:38:18.730
But all of this
definitional style

00:38:18.730 --> 00:38:22.399
would very much connect
these two or three--

00:38:22.399 --> 00:38:24.190
it's hard for me to
think of them as three,

00:38:24.190 --> 00:38:28.990
but certainly a multiple
view of these matters.

00:38:28.990 --> 00:38:31.780
I'd like to go still further
and talk about their internal--

00:38:31.780 --> 00:38:33.670
the way it appears to
practitioners of it.

00:38:33.670 --> 00:38:35.560
All of you as students
were practitioners.

00:38:35.560 --> 00:38:38.780
All of you now are practitioners
in some direction or another.

00:38:38.780 --> 00:38:42.310
And I think you will recognize,
feel sympathy with what I say,

00:38:42.310 --> 00:38:46.150
when I say that there are
a couple of apparently

00:38:46.150 --> 00:38:50.920
polar divisions, contradictions
opposites, which have to be

00:38:50.920 --> 00:38:52.810
united in each of these things.

00:38:52.810 --> 00:38:59.670
A curious but quite realistic
account of what people do.

00:38:59.670 --> 00:39:03.960
For example, the passive
absorption of information

00:39:03.960 --> 00:39:06.930
to learn a language, to learn
mathematical formulation,

00:39:06.930 --> 00:39:10.320
to learn the use of
a pen or a brush,

00:39:10.320 --> 00:39:13.110
to learn the language
of the stage, whatever

00:39:13.110 --> 00:39:17.820
it might be-- to learn the
notes of the scale and the keys.

00:39:17.820 --> 00:39:22.560
All of those things can be done
as a passive game where someone

00:39:22.560 --> 00:39:26.280
feeds the information-- a
book, a program, a person--

00:39:26.280 --> 00:39:27.926
and you gain it.

00:39:27.926 --> 00:39:31.650
But of the other side, activity
is indispensable, especially

00:39:31.650 --> 00:39:34.510
if to learn those things
with that beautiful word,

00:39:34.510 --> 00:39:35.550
"productivity."

00:39:35.550 --> 00:39:38.400
So it can again recombine
them for yourselves.

00:39:38.400 --> 00:39:40.710
We know that the people
who teach physics at MIT

00:39:40.710 --> 00:39:43.730
and everywhere else, that
the solving of problems--

00:39:43.730 --> 00:39:45.180
onerous as it may be--

00:39:45.180 --> 00:39:48.180
is a way to secure
the active knowledge

00:39:48.180 --> 00:39:50.100
of the things
passively observed,

00:39:50.100 --> 00:39:53.130
underlined in yellow
marker, the big propositions

00:39:53.130 --> 00:39:54.617
in the textbook.

00:39:54.617 --> 00:39:55.950
You've got to put them together.

00:39:55.950 --> 00:39:58.090
You've got to make
them work for you.

00:39:58.090 --> 00:40:00.790
You have to feel somewhat
what we say at home with them.

00:40:00.790 --> 00:40:03.150
Until that's happened,
no genuine understanding

00:40:03.150 --> 00:40:04.080
will come in.

00:40:04.080 --> 00:40:08.610
The answering of multiple-choice
tests in familiar language,

00:40:08.610 --> 00:40:10.920
choosing the right
one of six, is not

00:40:10.920 --> 00:40:13.110
a very powerful
test for science,

00:40:13.110 --> 00:40:15.444
for engineering, or for art.

00:40:15.444 --> 00:40:16.860
It may be good
enough for learning

00:40:16.860 --> 00:40:20.000
certain received
bodies of truth-- fine.

00:40:20.000 --> 00:40:21.480
I'm not against it entirely.

00:40:21.480 --> 00:40:24.030
But it is clearly-- in our day
it's a basis of controversy,

00:40:24.030 --> 00:40:26.370
and surely overused.

00:40:26.370 --> 00:40:29.010
So we have to combine the
passive and the active,

00:40:29.010 --> 00:40:33.870
and activity is the sine qua
non of the hand and the mind.

00:40:33.870 --> 00:40:44.840
The same similar dichotomy
goes between the analytic

00:40:44.840 --> 00:40:47.397
and the synthetic.

00:40:47.397 --> 00:40:49.480
It was said in popular
psychology only a few years

00:40:49.480 --> 00:40:51.280
ago-- it was quite
popular in the magazines--

00:40:51.280 --> 00:40:53.571
there were halves of the
brain, the left and the right.

00:40:53.571 --> 00:40:55.990
And one attended to
the holistic things,

00:40:55.990 --> 00:40:58.040
the organization, the concepts.

00:40:58.040 --> 00:41:03.460
And one attended to the details,
the rigorous logical thinking.

00:41:03.460 --> 00:41:06.100
One was suitable for
children, one for grown-ups,

00:41:06.100 --> 00:41:07.351
and that was the general idea.

00:41:07.351 --> 00:41:09.183
People who said that,
of course, were mostly

00:41:09.183 --> 00:41:11.309
writers, because they
understood writing very well.

00:41:11.309 --> 00:41:13.516
They knew how hard it was
to formulate good sentences

00:41:13.516 --> 00:41:14.170
and paragraphs.

00:41:14.170 --> 00:41:15.836
And they said, well,
in the kindergarten

00:41:15.836 --> 00:41:17.720
they can draw quite
well and make images,

00:41:17.720 --> 00:41:18.940
and that's enough for that.

00:41:18.940 --> 00:41:20.660
Of course, this is a caricature.

00:41:20.660 --> 00:41:22.150
It's a very bad proposition.

00:41:22.150 --> 00:41:24.910
Analytic and synthetic
again are together.

00:41:24.910 --> 00:41:27.790
The analyst indeed
may take things apart.

00:41:27.790 --> 00:41:30.880
But the tools he uses were
themselves synthesized

00:41:30.880 --> 00:41:33.880
by earlier designers
who synthesized

00:41:33.880 --> 00:41:38.380
the scalpel, or the
method of least squares,

00:41:38.380 --> 00:41:41.170
whatever it might be, by putting
together something new that

00:41:41.170 --> 00:41:42.710
had not existed before.

00:41:42.710 --> 00:41:46.570
So I reject that as a
harsh division of the world

00:41:46.570 --> 00:41:47.500
and say, no.

00:41:47.500 --> 00:41:50.230
Both halves of the
mind are necessary.

00:41:50.230 --> 00:41:53.410
All parts of the
mind, like both hands.

00:41:53.410 --> 00:42:00.050
And the unitarity
of this is visible,

00:42:00.050 --> 00:42:02.930
and visible in the
sense that we all

00:42:02.930 --> 00:42:05.960
know that for every geometrical
statement in Euclid,

00:42:05.960 --> 00:42:08.120
an algebraic equivalent
can be formed.

00:42:08.120 --> 00:42:09.490
And vice versa.

00:42:09.490 --> 00:42:12.830
The symbols of language the
symbols of x and y and plus,

00:42:12.830 --> 00:42:16.260
are no more foreign than
the lines and the angles.

00:42:16.260 --> 00:42:18.110
They're just different
ways of presentation,

00:42:18.110 --> 00:42:20.910
and they have a one
for one correspondence.

00:42:20.910 --> 00:42:22.460
Of course, the
symbolism of our world

00:42:22.460 --> 00:42:23.720
is so powerful now it extends.

00:42:23.720 --> 00:42:25.400
It goes beyond what
we can easily draw.

00:42:25.400 --> 00:42:26.649
You can do any dimensionality.

00:42:26.649 --> 00:42:29.480
I accept that and use it daily.

00:42:29.480 --> 00:42:32.190
But this is the issue.

00:42:32.190 --> 00:42:38.670
Now there is a feeling that
there's a difference in tone,

00:42:38.670 --> 00:42:40.260
and there is some difference.

00:42:40.260 --> 00:42:42.020
The scientists and
engineers seem serious.

00:42:42.020 --> 00:42:49.440
They're concerned with matters
of prosperity and penury.

00:42:49.440 --> 00:42:53.520
Or sometimes life and death.

00:42:53.520 --> 00:42:55.170
Or sometimes peace and war.

00:42:55.170 --> 00:42:56.970
And this seems more
serious, perhaps,

00:42:56.970 --> 00:43:00.180
than the more playful
activity of the artist.

00:43:00.180 --> 00:43:03.780
But I recommend you, thinking
through our language,

00:43:03.780 --> 00:43:07.290
the word "play" has
wide connotations.

00:43:07.290 --> 00:43:10.140
The player may play
Medea, or Hamlet.

00:43:10.140 --> 00:43:12.420
Hardly a lighthearted affair.

00:43:12.420 --> 00:43:15.990
Something plumbing the
depths of human behavior.

00:43:15.990 --> 00:43:18.010
Lightness of heart is
not the whole story.

00:43:18.010 --> 00:43:21.120
The primary colors, and
the cube, the sphere,

00:43:21.120 --> 00:43:23.970
are fine things for
the elementary school,

00:43:23.970 --> 00:43:26.400
indispensable for
the small child.

00:43:26.400 --> 00:43:28.830
But they are not the
whole story of play.

00:43:28.830 --> 00:43:32.070
Play, which is unique
in the vertebrates,

00:43:32.070 --> 00:43:36.960
and best developed in those
closest to us is profound.

00:43:36.960 --> 00:43:39.367
I worked a lot
with Charles Ames,

00:43:39.367 --> 00:43:41.700
who said that one of the most
serious activities in life

00:43:41.700 --> 00:43:46.170
was play, and he demonstrated
it every day by what he did.

00:43:46.170 --> 00:43:48.120
Play is a means of--

00:43:48.120 --> 00:43:49.860
clearly what the word implies--

00:43:49.860 --> 00:43:51.370
model making of the future.

00:43:51.370 --> 00:43:54.270
What would it be to do like
that, not to carry it out,

00:43:54.270 --> 00:43:56.010
not to make that
full investment?

00:43:56.010 --> 00:44:01.050
But to acquire the mastery in a
more benign, a more acceptable,

00:44:01.050 --> 00:44:03.330
a more easily
entered environment.

00:44:03.330 --> 00:44:04.620
And that is what play is.

00:44:04.620 --> 00:44:11.190
And since you cannot master
the world in a stroke,

00:44:11.190 --> 00:44:14.880
play is an indispensable
feature of human behavior.

00:44:14.880 --> 00:44:19.290
From word play of the infant
and the mother in the home,

00:44:19.290 --> 00:44:22.770
to the most profound
thought of an Einstein

00:44:22.770 --> 00:44:28.710
or a composer of great value,
Bach in the course of his work,

00:44:28.710 --> 00:44:31.800
as any exposure to
art demonstrates

00:44:31.800 --> 00:44:33.510
that these things have.

00:44:33.510 --> 00:44:35.250
And the scientists,
too, must have it.

00:44:35.250 --> 00:44:38.340
I'd like to say a word for
playful engineering, which

00:44:38.340 --> 00:44:40.800
I think is an important
issue of our time

00:44:40.800 --> 00:44:45.360
that we have not
yet fully engaged.

00:44:45.360 --> 00:44:46.570
It's not new.

00:44:46.570 --> 00:44:48.870
There always has
been such a thing,

00:44:48.870 --> 00:44:52.690
but I'd like to show an
example of it in a slide.

00:44:52.690 --> 00:44:54.190
I hope you can
recognize this slide.

00:44:54.190 --> 00:44:55.530
It's a very unusual slide.

00:44:55.530 --> 00:44:57.690
It was made in 1936.

00:44:57.690 --> 00:45:01.590
It shows one of the greatest
works of playful engineering,

00:45:01.590 --> 00:45:03.240
the Eiffel Tower.

00:45:03.240 --> 00:45:07.020
You can perhaps recognize the
Avenue Chaillot down here,

00:45:07.020 --> 00:45:09.450
and the arches of the Eiffel
Tower there, all heavily

00:45:09.450 --> 00:45:14.340
illuminated in this time
exposure from 1936, where

00:45:14.340 --> 00:45:18.020
of course, what you see is a
pyrotechnic display, fireworks,

00:45:18.020 --> 00:45:20.297
off all parts of
the Eiffel Tower.

00:45:20.297 --> 00:45:22.380
But of course, the Eiffel
Tower was built strictly

00:45:22.380 --> 00:45:25.950
as a playful operation,
a decoration for Paris

00:45:25.950 --> 00:45:31.320
from the 1889 World's Fair there
by a great structural engineer.

00:45:31.320 --> 00:45:34.930
It worked 10,000 tons of
wrought iron, not of steel,

00:45:34.930 --> 00:45:36.660
of wrought iron and rivets.

00:45:36.660 --> 00:45:40.500
And made playfully.

00:45:40.500 --> 00:45:43.384
Of course, managing
to extract a fee

00:45:43.384 --> 00:45:45.300
from the people who come
so it can keep going,

00:45:45.300 --> 00:45:46.380
keep itself painted.

00:45:46.380 --> 00:45:48.960
And indeed, pay the shareholders
quite a considerable income,

00:45:48.960 --> 00:45:50.610
I understand, over the years.

00:45:50.610 --> 00:45:53.850
But nevertheless,
that's its foundation.

00:45:53.850 --> 00:45:57.990
It doesn't do anything but
stand there magnificently.

00:45:57.990 --> 00:46:00.600
At first, of course, attacked
by half the artists and writers

00:46:00.600 --> 00:46:01.710
in Paris.

00:46:01.710 --> 00:46:06.750
And now inseparable from
the nature of the city.

00:46:06.750 --> 00:46:09.360
And I'm sure that you could
not possibly tear it down

00:46:09.360 --> 00:46:12.570
without a major
revolution swirling

00:46:12.570 --> 00:46:15.660
about the base of the tower.

00:46:15.660 --> 00:46:20.046
Now playful engineering is not
enough practiced in our time.

00:46:20.046 --> 00:46:20.920
It is practiced some.

00:46:20.920 --> 00:46:21.794
We all know about it.

00:46:21.794 --> 00:46:24.480
We know about it in sports
where it's become a specialty,

00:46:24.480 --> 00:46:29.050
and almost lost its playful call
because of its heavy rewards.

00:46:29.050 --> 00:46:35.430
And at MIT, from the impromptu
and very light-hearted hack,

00:46:35.430 --> 00:46:39.310
of which I need not
give you any examples,

00:46:39.310 --> 00:46:43.290
through the pedal-driven
aircraft of great renown,

00:46:43.290 --> 00:46:45.000
to the wonderful
stroboscopic work

00:46:45.000 --> 00:46:48.300
of Papa Flash, the
late Doc Edgerton.

00:46:48.300 --> 00:46:52.080
To the sophisticated work,
whose 25th anniversary

00:46:52.080 --> 00:46:53.870
is being celebrated
tomorrow at the

00:46:53.870 --> 00:46:57.990
of Center of Advanced
Visual Studies, which

00:46:57.990 --> 00:47:01.380
I remember for sort of three
or four works of grand works.

00:47:01.380 --> 00:47:03.830
Not single works,
but whole campaigns

00:47:03.830 --> 00:47:05.310
of playful engineering.

00:47:05.310 --> 00:47:09.180
Mechanisms of extraordinary
wit and pleasure.

00:47:09.180 --> 00:47:11.790
The use of aerial
photography as an art form,

00:47:11.790 --> 00:47:15.120
not as a geodetic study or
a way of measuring crops.

00:47:15.120 --> 00:47:20.280
And sky art, lasers and balloons
on grand scale just beginning

00:47:20.280 --> 00:47:23.510
in this world in
the last few years.

00:47:23.510 --> 00:47:26.760
And I am quite sure also
that the Palais du Louvre

00:47:26.760 --> 00:47:30.930
has been recently subjected to
something which is architecture

00:47:30.930 --> 00:47:34.890
and playful in its qualities,
and at the same time profoundly

00:47:34.890 --> 00:47:35.910
purposeful.

00:47:35.910 --> 00:47:40.980
And I'm sure that IM Pei and
Dean Mitchell will describe

00:47:40.980 --> 00:47:44.100
something about that in
their talk of recent work

00:47:44.100 --> 00:47:46.450
just to come.

00:47:46.450 --> 00:47:49.010
Now I'm going to close.

00:47:49.010 --> 00:47:56.200
And I want to close with a
somewhat novel end, which

00:47:56.200 --> 00:47:58.720
I had not really planned,
but it was forced out of me

00:47:58.720 --> 00:48:02.050
as I went through these
thoughts in the last few weeks.

00:48:02.050 --> 00:48:04.290
I see one danger.

00:48:04.290 --> 00:48:06.940
The danger is an
extraordinarily clear one,

00:48:06.940 --> 00:48:09.670
and I think we have found
the best way to avoid it.

00:48:09.670 --> 00:48:12.504
I'll tell you what that is
and see if you agree with me.

00:48:12.504 --> 00:48:13.420
Please think about it.

00:48:13.420 --> 00:48:14.740
It's something novel.

00:48:14.740 --> 00:48:16.840
It's something of great
importance to all of us.

00:48:16.840 --> 00:48:21.220
And it needs
widespread attention.

00:48:21.220 --> 00:48:25.820
And that is it's clear
that nowadays art produces

00:48:25.820 --> 00:48:28.130
its own truths.

00:48:28.130 --> 00:48:36.200
The artwork is not judged by its
comparison with another canon

00:48:36.200 --> 00:48:37.400
necessarily.

00:48:37.400 --> 00:48:39.330
Yes, it might be of the
School of Beaux-Arts.

00:48:39.330 --> 00:48:40.430
It might not.

00:48:40.430 --> 00:48:41.800
The schools are various.

00:48:41.800 --> 00:48:43.910
The techniques are innumerable.

00:48:43.910 --> 00:48:45.770
The subject matter,
the minute particulars

00:48:45.770 --> 00:48:47.220
are as wide as can be.

00:48:47.220 --> 00:48:49.220
And I think we should
accept that as the freedom

00:48:49.220 --> 00:48:50.656
that the artist has.

00:48:50.656 --> 00:48:52.280
Of course, he may
follow a strict form.

00:48:52.280 --> 00:48:53.238
He may wish to do that.

00:48:53.238 --> 00:48:55.700
Or he may break loose
into something quite new.

00:48:55.700 --> 00:48:57.980
But in any case, there's
no other criterion,

00:48:57.980 --> 00:48:59.120
no external one.

00:48:59.120 --> 00:49:01.790
The scientists must--
the input is what counts.

00:49:01.790 --> 00:49:04.280
The data must be
reliably presented.

00:49:04.280 --> 00:49:06.200
The engineer, the
object must work

00:49:06.200 --> 00:49:07.970
for the purpose of
which it was intended

00:49:07.970 --> 00:49:10.040
or some purpose close to that.

00:49:10.040 --> 00:49:12.680
But the artist, it is
the output that counts.

00:49:12.680 --> 00:49:14.720
Does it work for the
purpose he intended it--

00:49:14.720 --> 00:49:17.372
to amuse, to entertain, to
teach, whatever it might be?

00:49:17.372 --> 00:49:18.830
It doesn't matter
how he got there.

00:49:18.830 --> 00:49:22.700
He isn't responsible to show
you the data on which he worked.

00:49:22.700 --> 00:49:24.190
And on the other
hand, this leads

00:49:24.190 --> 00:49:27.420
to a great danger, of which
a very tiny example my wife,

00:49:27.420 --> 00:49:29.480
Phyllis Morrison,
helped me produce

00:49:29.480 --> 00:49:32.700
for the next last slide.

00:49:32.700 --> 00:49:33.760
Well.

00:49:33.760 --> 00:49:35.880
It's the Eiffel
Tower again, right?

00:49:35.880 --> 00:49:37.500
In fact, it's the same--

00:49:37.500 --> 00:49:39.930
you'll see it's the same
pyrotechnic picture.

00:49:39.930 --> 00:49:43.720
But spontaneously, with the
matter of a few seconds,

00:49:43.720 --> 00:49:47.370
Phyllis has managed to
give a kind of impression

00:49:47.370 --> 00:49:50.100
that there is a colorful
pyrotechnic display coming

00:49:50.100 --> 00:49:51.750
from the great tower.

00:49:51.750 --> 00:49:54.210
And of course, you are
all well sophisticated

00:49:54.210 --> 00:49:56.270
enough with images,
and with now markers,

00:49:56.270 --> 00:49:58.020
to recognize that of
course it has nothing

00:49:58.020 --> 00:49:59.222
to the Eiffel Tower at all.

00:49:59.222 --> 00:50:00.430
What does it have to do with?

00:50:00.430 --> 00:50:03.390
It has to do with a
35-millimeter slide that

00:50:03.390 --> 00:50:06.840
represented the Eiffel Tower
in 1936, which she has then

00:50:06.840 --> 00:50:12.270
modified with just a very
small spontaneous artistic work

00:50:12.270 --> 00:50:13.610
to make the point here.

00:50:13.610 --> 00:50:16.212
But the image, however,
is itself, it is there.

00:50:16.212 --> 00:50:17.170
And it means something.

00:50:17.170 --> 00:50:19.009
It's actually quite striking.

00:50:19.009 --> 00:50:20.550
And now the trouble
is we make images

00:50:20.550 --> 00:50:25.500
so well today, we make so many,
we can make them so seamlessly.

00:50:25.500 --> 00:50:27.810
We can change their
appearance in every way.

00:50:27.810 --> 00:50:31.200
Not only visual images, but even
speaking more metaphorically,

00:50:31.200 --> 00:50:32.820
auditory and the rest.

00:50:32.820 --> 00:50:37.832
That there is a cut-off
between the world represented--

00:50:37.832 --> 00:50:38.790
may not be represented.

00:50:38.790 --> 00:50:41.490
But even those things when
they are represented, well

00:50:41.490 --> 00:50:44.490
represented the image are no
longer good maps of each other.

00:50:44.490 --> 00:50:46.830
The artist's intervention
is so powerful

00:50:46.830 --> 00:50:48.720
that he has ended that.

00:50:48.720 --> 00:50:51.030
Now that is not new to us.

00:50:51.030 --> 00:50:54.240
We all experience it
internally, and it is probably

00:50:54.240 --> 00:50:57.960
indispensable to the human mind
in the way we have evolved.

00:50:57.960 --> 00:51:01.000
It is what we call
the world of dream.

00:51:01.000 --> 00:51:04.050
In dream, visual
images are produced

00:51:04.050 --> 00:51:07.800
which have maybe some
semblance of your experience.

00:51:07.800 --> 00:51:09.340
Sometimes not, perhaps.

00:51:09.340 --> 00:51:11.835
But when they have
semblance, we know very well

00:51:11.835 --> 00:51:17.590
that those dreams are not the
relatively close approximations

00:51:17.590 --> 00:51:18.910
to what is going on.

00:51:18.910 --> 00:51:20.830
That the eyes which
are always limited,

00:51:20.830 --> 00:51:24.950
and the video screen which is
equally and similarly limited,

00:51:24.950 --> 00:51:28.310
bring to you approximations
of the real world.

00:51:28.310 --> 00:51:32.050
Now what I am trying to say
is a danger that if we have,

00:51:32.050 --> 00:51:35.260
as we do have, the
greatest 20th century

00:51:35.260 --> 00:51:37.060
arts, the one that
attracts most people,

00:51:37.060 --> 00:51:39.400
and I think has gradually,
gradually increased

00:51:39.400 --> 00:51:41.920
its salience is the cinema.

00:51:41.920 --> 00:51:44.650
And I strongly suspect
the 21st century

00:51:44.650 --> 00:51:47.740
will make the video equivalent
of cinema with its music,

00:51:47.740 --> 00:51:50.800
with its changes, which
is probable 3D nature.

00:51:50.800 --> 00:51:53.440
And its motions and
change will probably

00:51:53.440 --> 00:51:56.480
transcend all previous
visual experiences,

00:51:56.480 --> 00:51:59.866
will make an enormous
push upon the world.

00:51:59.866 --> 00:52:01.240
Whereas the capital
of this world

00:52:01.240 --> 00:52:04.210
is only going to double
or triple and plateau out.

00:52:04.210 --> 00:52:05.680
There isn't room for more.

00:52:05.680 --> 00:52:07.745
There's not room in
the atmospheric balance

00:52:07.745 --> 00:52:09.740
and the heat sources for more.

00:52:09.740 --> 00:52:12.670
These sources of information
and image are enormous,

00:52:12.670 --> 00:52:14.350
and they will continue.

00:52:14.350 --> 00:52:16.660
And the real question
is that we not

00:52:16.660 --> 00:52:20.920
be swamped in a dream world,
where we no longer pay

00:52:20.920 --> 00:52:23.350
attention to what the
representation was

00:52:23.350 --> 00:52:26.080
of, but only the nature of
the presentation itself.

00:52:26.080 --> 00:52:27.870
That is, after all,
the artist's task.

00:52:27.870 --> 00:52:31.390
And I cannot think of a better
way of understanding that

00:52:31.390 --> 00:52:35.050
problem of conquering it than
by having people who know both

00:52:35.050 --> 00:52:38.350
sides of the operation, who
have studied the arts to see how

00:52:38.350 --> 00:52:41.530
much you can do to make
entertaining and wonderful

00:52:41.530 --> 00:52:44.860
things that didn't exist,
but also how much you can do

00:52:44.860 --> 00:52:48.045
to represent scrupulously
and meticulously the data

00:52:48.045 --> 00:52:52.660
of the real world, the actual
performance of engineering

00:52:52.660 --> 00:52:56.740
without hyperbole and public
relations and the rest.

00:52:56.740 --> 00:52:58.540
To take only one
side or stay away

00:52:58.540 --> 00:53:01.030
from the other,
that will not work,

00:53:01.030 --> 00:53:04.360
because people are too clever
and they'll transcend it.

00:53:04.360 --> 00:53:06.450
What is the best is
to expose them both.

00:53:06.450 --> 00:53:10.060
Come right up front and
say, yes, this can be done,

00:53:10.060 --> 00:53:13.990
the artist has one direction,
the engineer scientist

00:53:13.990 --> 00:53:14.920
has another.

00:53:14.920 --> 00:53:18.090
Both recognize their
enormous similarity,

00:53:18.090 --> 00:53:21.580
but by their mutual
understanding

00:53:21.580 --> 00:53:25.630
we can have a world which both
contains dreams, but is not

00:53:25.630 --> 00:53:27.044
solely based on it.

00:53:27.044 --> 00:53:29.710
And I think that's the only kind
of a world which is safe for us

00:53:29.710 --> 00:53:34.340
to try to carry on the legacy
of the canyon at Olduvai.

00:53:34.340 --> 00:53:35.240
Thank you.

00:53:35.240 --> 00:53:38.290
[APPLAUSE]

